THOUGHT: Now I understand the vulnerability completely:

1. `AddProperty(const aiString *pInput, ...)` calls `AddBinaryProperty(pInput, pInput->length + 1 + 4, ...)`.
2. `AddBinaryProperty` does `memcpy(pcNew->mData, pInput, pSizeInBytes)` where `pSizeInBytes = pInput->length + 5`.
3. `aiString` is `{ uint32_t length; char data[MAXLEN]; }` = 4 + 1024 = 1028 bytes.
4. If `pInput->length >= 1024`, then `pSizeInBytes >= 1029`, which reads past the end of the 1028-byte `aiString` object on the stack.

In the MDL loader:
```cpp
aiString szFile;
const size_t iLen = strlen((const char *)szCurrent);
size_t iLen2 = iLen + 1;
iLen2 = iLen2 > MAXLEN ? MAXLEN : iLen2;
memcpy(szFile.data, (const char *)szCurrent, iLen2);
szFile.length = (ai_uint32)iLen;
```

If `iLen >= 1024`, then `szFile.length = iLen >= 1024`, and `AddProperty(&szFile, ...)` will call `AddBinaryProperty(&szFile, iLen + 5, ...)` which does `memcpy(pcNew->mData, &szFile, iLen + 5)`. Since `szFile` is only 1028 bytes on the stack, reading `iLen + 5 >= 1029` bytes reads past the end of the stack object.

